<h3>3. Thariq&rsquo;s first argument — capability overhang: the ability is there, but nobody knows how to use it</h3>

<p>The first concept running through Thariq&rsquo;s talk is <strong>capability overhang</strong>. A capability already exists in the model, but users and product teams have not yet discovered how to draw it out. Evaluating a new model only with the same prompts, the same system prompt, and the same tool contracts as the previous one can lead you to underestimate its real capability.</p>

<p>The Pokémon example he gives is intuitive. Ask a general chat model to find every item among a huge list of names that satisfies a particular string condition, and it can get it wrong working from memory and reasoning alone. An agent like Claude Code, however, can fetch the data, write a script, and run a filter to verify the answer. The performance gain here comes not from &ldquo;having memorized more&rdquo; but from <strong>reshaping the problem into a form that tools can attack</strong>.</p>

<div class="note">
    <span class="nh">Practical reading</span>
    A <strong>model</strong> and a <strong>model plus tools plus permissions plus context plus a verification loop</strong> are not the same system. Before concluding that AI cannot do something, check first whether there is a harness that would reveal the capability.
</div>

<p>A 2026 architectural analysis of Claude Code reinforces this view. The study treats the surrounding systems — shell tools, permissions, context management, MCP, plugins, skills, hooks, subagents — as an important design space rather than a simple model-call loop. Put in Thariq&rsquo;s terms, there is a <strong>gap between the ceiling of the model&rsquo;s capability and the capability an actual product draws out of it</strong>.</p>

<h3>4. Unhobbling Claude — do not bind a strong model with the previous model&rsquo;s rules</h3>

<p>Thariq frames making good use of a new model less as plain prompt engineering and more as a problem of <strong>unhobbling</strong> — releasing the constraints tied around the model&rsquo;s ankles. There are two important cases here.</p>

<h4>4-1. The claim that the system prompt was cut down</h4>

<p>According to write-ups summarizing Thariq&rsquo;s public talk, the Claude Code team stripped out a substantial portion of the system prompt as the model got stronger, and the figure <strong>about 80%</strong> was mentioned in the talk. The reason is paradoxical. The lists of prohibitions and examples that helped a weak model can, on a strong model, act as a boundary on the solutions it will consider.</p>

<p>That said, this <strong>80%</strong> is not confirmed as an official change figure stated in Anthropic&rsquo;s product documentation. So it is right to treat it as <strong>a claim made in Thariq&rsquo;s public talk</strong> rather than assert it as a precise official company metric. Even so, the point stands. If you adopt a new model while keeping intact the rules you piled up to prevent past failures, you can inherit the old model&rsquo;s limits in the new one.</p>

<h4>4-2. Even the same question-asking tool behaved differently across model generations</h4>

<p>Thariq cites the tool for asking the user questions as an example. By his account, on the previous generation of models it took a lot of fiddling to get the tool called reliably; on later models, instructing them to ask about the spec produced something that behaved like a real interview; and with Fable it went as far as organizing the questions and results into a more structured form.</p>

<p>The point is not which version wins. <strong>Even with an identical tool spec, the interaction pattern changes when the model changes.</strong> A model upgrade is therefore not a drop-in swap but a system change in which prompts, tool descriptions, permissions, and verification methods all have to be re-experimented with.</p>

<h3>5. The core frame — the map is not the territory</h3>

<p>The <strong>map</strong> is the representation the user handed to the AI. Prompts, PRDs, specs, CLAUDE.md, example code, design guides, meeting notes, and tickets all belong here. The <strong>territory</strong> is the reality where the work actually happens. Legacy code, hidden data constraints, deployment environments, customer behavior, an organization&rsquo;s tacit rules, incident history, and legal limits are the territory.</p>

<p>The problem is that the map is always incomplete. People do not write down everything they know, they obviously cannot write what they do not know, and what they never considered does not even become a question. An agent does not stop every time it hits one of those blanks. <strong>It forms an assumption and keeps going.</strong> The stronger the model, the farther it can travel on top of that assumption.</p>

