<h3>8. Pattern 1 — Blindspot pass: do not implement right away; find the blind spots first</h3>

<p>In an unfamiliar codebase, the most dangerous move is for the person who does not know it to lock down a detailed spec first. Thariq&rsquo;s first pattern has the agent read the codebase before implementation and find <strong>the problems the user has not yet been able to ask about</strong>.</p>

<div class="note">
    <span class="nh">Prompt pattern — rendered in English while preserving the sense of the original</span>
    I want to add a new SSO auth provider to Acme, but I have never worked in the auth module. Do not implement it yet — run a blindspot pass. Find what I do not know I do not know about this area of the codebase, explain each item, and then tell me how to write a better prompt for the implementation.
</div>

<p>This pattern is not &ldquo;give me the answer&rdquo; but <strong>&ldquo;find the flaws in my question.&rdquo;</strong> It is especially strong in systems like ERP, where business rules are spread across code and data. Constraints absent from the request form — state transitions, cancellation rules, accounting close, customer-specific extension columns — can surface before implementation.</p>

<h3>9. Pattern 2 — Teach me my unknowns: start by learning the language of the area you do not know</h3>

<p>The second artifact is sharper when the subject of the work is not code. Thariq uses color grading as an example. If you do not know the terminology and the judgment criteria, you cannot ask precisely for the result you want. So instead of telling the AI to produce the result, you ask it to <strong>first teach you the axes and vocabulary an expert sees</strong>.</p>

<div class="note">
    <span class="nh">Key point</span>
    To write a good prompt, you first have to know what distinctions matter in that field. Before it is an executor, AI can be <strong>a tutor for building your questions</strong>.
</div>

<h3>10. Pattern 3 — Four design directions: do not explain your preference; find it by making options collide</h3>

<p>Users often know good from bad once they see the result, yet cannot put their taste into words before starting. Thariq has the model build not four subtly different options but <strong>four directions with sharply different premises</strong>, in real HTML. The user reacts and chooses, and the choice becomes a constraint on the next iteration.</p>

<div class="note">
    <span class="nh">Prompt pattern</span>
    I cannot describe my visual taste for this screen precisely. Instead of four similar variations, build four design directions with different philosophies on a single HTML page and show me. I will compare and react.
</div>

<p>This is a way to find <strong>unknown knowns</strong>. The user already holds the preference but cannot articulate it. The prototype pulls that tacit criterion out into the open.</p>

<h3>11. Pattern 4 — Mock before you wire: buy cheap failure before touching the real system</h3>

<p>Thariq&rsquo;s fourth pattern builds a <strong>single-file HTML mock</strong> before wiring into the real app. It validates the flow and the screens before entering expensive work such as data contracts, backend connections, and state management.</p>

<p>The important part is not &ldquo;also build a prototype.&rdquo; It is <strong>discover the user&rsquo;s hidden criteria before the irreversible cost grows</strong>. A 30-minute failure before implementation can prevent a three-day rework after it.</p>

<h3>12. Pattern 5 — Brainstorm the intervention: widen the intervention points before picking one solution</h3>

<p>When users spot a problem, they commonly write the solution that occurred to them straight into the prompt. But at that moment the solution space narrows. Thariq has the model read the codebase first and find <strong>10 possible intervention points</strong>, laid out from the cheapest to the most ambitious.</p>

<div class="note">
    <span class="nh">Practical meaning</span>
    &ldquo;Explore the intervention points that could reduce this problem&rdquo; comes before &ldquo;modify this function.&rdquo; It is the step that verifies whether the location the user picked really is the optimal place to change.
</div>

