<p>This contrast shows why Four design directions, Mock, Brainstorm, and Reference belong to the same family. All four ask the model not for an immediate answer but to <strong>externalize a searchable space so the human can react to it.</strong> In other words, they raise the <strong>quality of the search space</strong> before the performance of answer generation.</p>

<h3>13. Pattern 6 — The interview: the AI interviews the user</h3>

<p>Once a direction is set, you have the model ask the questions instead. What matters here is not &ldquo;any questions?&rdquo; Thariq&rsquo;s pattern asks <strong>one at a time</strong>, lets the user&rsquo;s answer change the next question, and asks <strong>the questions that most change the architecture first</strong>.</p>

<div class="note">
    <span class="nh">Prompt pattern</span>
    Interview me before implementing. Ask one question at a time and change your follow-ups based on my answers. Order the questions so the ones whose answers most change the architecture come first. When you have enough, summarize with what is decided, what is undecided, and what you assumed kept separate.
</div>

<p>The goal of this approach is not to increase the number of questions. It is <strong>to move the questions with high information value earlier</strong>. Decisions that are expensive to reverse — who authenticates, who owns the data, the multi-company scope, consistency during outages — get settled first.</p>

<h3>14. Pattern 7 — Point at a reference: give a working map rather than an explanation</h3>

<p>If the behavior you want is hard to describe precisely in words, hand over an implementation that already works. Thariq&rsquo;s published example is the situation of <strong>reimplementing the semantics of a Rust crate in TypeScript</strong>. It does not matter that the languages differ. You build a semantics map first, confirm what must be preserved, and then port it.</p>

<p>A good reference carries more information than dozens of sentences, because error handling, ordering, boundary conditions, invariants, and the feel of the API are all present in the actual behavior. But copying is not the answer. You have to <strong>separate the semantics to preserve from the surface to adapt to the current environment</strong>.</p>

<h3>15. Pattern 8 — The tweakable plan: put the changeable decisions at the front of the plan</h3>

<p>This is an important pattern that is easily dropped from Thariq&rsquo;s companion guide. Rather than making the implementation plan a plain task list, it puts <strong>the decisions the user can adjust</strong> up front. Items whose direction has large downstream impact — the data model, type interfaces, user-facing behavior — are shown first, and mechanical refactoring is pushed to the back.</p>

<div class="note">
    <span class="nh">Why it matters</span>
    What a human needs to review is not every line of code. It is the <strong>decision space</strong>. When a plan document is packed with mechanical work, the design decisions that actually need changing get missed.
</div>

<h3>16. Pattern 9 — Implementation notes: do not hide deviations from the plan</h3>

<p>No matter how well you prepare, reality collides with the plan during implementation. The danger with a strong agent is not deviating from the plan itself but <strong>rationalizing the deviation on its own and continuing without making it visible</strong>. Thariq has it maintain a separate implementation-notes file.</p>

<div class="note">
    <span class="nh">Prompt pattern</span>
    Maintain an implementation notes file. If an edge case forces you off the plan, take the more conservative of the available options and record under Deviations what changed, why, and what you assumed. Unless it is a fatal blocker, keep going.
</div>

<p>These notes mean more than a log. They are <strong>the mechanism for updating the map</strong>, turning the first task&rsquo;s unknowns into the next task&rsquo;s knowns. With long-horizon agents in particular, you have to leave behind decision provenance, not just the result.</p>

<h3>17. Pattern 10 — Buy-in doc: turn the implementation result into a unit of organizational decision-making</h3>

<p>This is the second most frequently dropped item of the 11 Thariq published. When implementation is done, the prototype, spec, and implementation notes are not left scattered but packaged into <strong>a single buy-in document you can share straight to Slack</strong>. And the demo leads, ahead of the explanation.</p>

<p>This is the step that converts individual productivity into organizational productivity. A result the AI produced quickly has no value if the team never adopts it. Binding the demo, the reasons for the decisions, the scope of the change, and the remaining risks into one document lowers <strong>the cost of review and agreement</strong>.</p>

<h3>18. Pattern 11 — Quiz me before I merge: code you cannot explain is not yet your code</h3>

<p>The last one is a quiz. Thariq has the model build an HTML report from the diff that explains the context of the change, the intuition, and what was actually implemented, then has it check with a quiz whether the user understood.</p>

<div class="note">
    <span class="nh">Prompt pattern</span>
    Based on this diff, build an HTML report that lets me fully understand the change. Explain the context, the core intuition, and what you actually did, and add a quiz at the end. Re-explain anything I get wrong and give me a variation of the question. Do not consider it done before I understand it well enough.
</div>

<p>This is no educational gimmick. As AI-generated code grows, <strong>&ldquo;do the tests pass?&rdquo;</strong> alone is not enough as the review question. Ownership only appears once you also confirm <strong>&ldquo;can the operator explain the failure modes and how to recover?&rdquo;</strong></p>

