<h3>20. Thariq&rsquo;s second big message — when the cost of production drops, the trade-offs have to be recalculated</h3>

<p>The latter part of Thariq&rsquo;s talk goes beyond prompting technique. He recalls that while running a startup in the past he always had to pick one thing. Polish the app, or experiment with a new feature — doing one meant deferring the other. Because the cost of making software was high.</p>

<p>When a strong coding agent like Fable drives the cost of work down sharply, you have to ask again whether keeping the old priority scheme intact is rational. Thariq&rsquo;s stance is closer to <strong>&ldquo;do not believe too quickly that there is a trade-off.&rdquo;</strong> Before choosing one of two, check whether you can try both, and which experiments used to be expensive but are cheap now.</p>

<p>At this point his &ldquo;be unreasonable&rdquo; is different from recklessness. It is the claim that <strong>you should not accept old constraints as facts once the cost function has changed</strong>.</p>

<h3>21. &ldquo;I loved coding, but I cannot go back&rdquo; — mourning the change</h3>

<p>Thariq does not only celebrate the productivity of coding agents. He says he loved writing code by hand, rotating a codebase in his head, and debugging late into the night. At the same time he remembers the cost of failing for weeks on end and of most projects not going the way he wanted.</p>

<p>So the conclusion is not a simple &ldquo;AI replaces developers.&rdquo; <strong>The feeling of losing part of a way of working you loved is real, but going back to the past after the mode of production has changed is not the answer.</strong> The message is that you have to pass through the change itself and build new forms of ownership and understanding.</p>

<h3>22. How far does outside research reinforce Thariq&rsquo;s argument</h3>

<h4>22-1. Agents are advancing toward performing longer tasks</h4>

<p>Research in the METR line reports a trend in which the time horizon of tasks frontier AI can complete successfully is growing fast. You cannot generalize that result directly to every real-world project, but it does reinforce Thariq&rsquo;s concern that <strong>models autonomously explore longer task paths</strong>.</p>

<h4>22-2. Agent performance cannot be explained by model performance alone</h4>

<p>The architectural analysis of Claude Code shows that tools, permissions, context, extension mechanisms, and subagents are core elements of the system&rsquo;s capability. This connects directly to the capability overhang and unhobbling arguments. <strong>What a model can do and what a product actually permits and encourages are different things.</strong></p>

<h4>22-3. Productivity metrics and value metrics have to be kept apart</h4>

<p>Research on coding-agent adoption observes an increase in development output but warns that the number of merged PRs does not by itself mean customer value or maintenance quality. Thariq&rsquo;s message lands on the same point. <strong>Even when the cost of building comes down, the cost of judging what to build does not disappear.</strong></p>

