<h3>24. Applied to Principal Researcher Youngjin Kwak&rsquo;s GitHub WIKI and Douzone&rsquo;s development organization</h3>

<p>Thariq&rsquo;s frame applies more to <strong>organizational knowledge systems</strong> than to individual prompting technique. ERP development in particular is a place where reading the code alone will not tell you the territory, because business rules, customer-specific exceptions, DB state, deployment history, incident response, and the judgment of the person in charge are scattered across many systems and people.</p>

<div class="tbl-wrap">
<table>
    <thead><tr><th>Thariq&rsquo;s pattern</th><th>Applied to ERP and the development organization</th><th>Turned into a WIKI asset</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr><td>Blindspot pass</td><td>Explore existing rules, states, DB, customer extensions, and incident history before filing a development request</td><td>List of unconfirmed constraints</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Teach me my unknowns</td><td>Learn the core terminology and decision axes of an unfamiliar business domain</td><td>Domain Primer</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Design directions / Mock</td><td>Compare shared UI and Agent screens quickly under several premises</td><td>Prototype Decision</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Brainstorm intervention</td><td>Compare rule, API, DB, batch, and UI intervention points beyond the requested edit location</td><td>Option Map</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Interview</td><td>Confirm with consultants, PMs, and developers starting from the questions with the largest architectural impact</td><td>Decision Record</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Reference</td><td>Compare existing customer implementations, standard modules, and other products&rsquo; behavior at the level of semantics</td><td>Semantic Reference</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Tweakable plan</td><td>Separate the data model, state scheme, and API contracts from mechanical edits</td><td>Reviewable Plan</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Implementation notes</td><td>Record deviations from the plan, production data exceptions, and conservative choices</td><td>Deviation Log</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Buy-in doc</td><td>Package the demo, decisions, and risks for sharing with the executive, PM, and related Units</td><td>Decision Brief</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Quiz</td><td>Have the responsible developer explain an AI-generated change down to the incident and recovery view</td><td>Ownership Check</td></tr>
    </tbody>
</table>
</div>

<p>Here the GitHub WIKI becomes not a mere document repository but <strong>a mechanism for promoting unknowns into knowns</strong>. An exception discovered in implementation notes gets documented, the document&rsquo;s core concepts get linked into the Knowledge Graph, and the next agent reads that map again. Then the trial and error of the first project becomes up-front context for the next one.</p>

<div class="note">
    <span class="nh">The most important conclusion from this wiki&rsquo;s point of view</span>
    <strong>A good knowledge graph is not only about connecting facts you already know. It has to make the unknowns discovered in past work reusable as known context for the next piece of work.</strong>
</div>

<h3>25. An operating protocol for practice — start the next agent task like this</h3>

<ol>
    <li><strong>A Blindspot pass before giving instructions</strong> — find the constraints you missed in the code, documents, and history first.</li>
    <li><strong>Teach me my unknowns for an unfamiliar domain</strong> — learn the terminology and the axes of judgment.</li>
    <li><strong>Four directions and a mock when preferences are vague</strong> — do not force the spec into words.</li>
    <li><strong>Explore intervention points before locking a solution</strong> — compare change locations broadly.</li>
    <li><strong>Interview starting from the questions with the largest architectural impact</strong> — one at a time, changing the next question based on the answer.</li>
    <li><strong>A semantics map when a good existing implementation exists</strong> — port the semantics and invariants, not the surface.</li>
    <li><strong>Plans with tweakable decisions first</strong> — put the decisions a human can change up front.</li>
    <li><strong>Record deviations during implementation</strong> — do not hide departures from the plan or assumptions.</li>
    <li><strong>A buy-in doc when done</strong> — put the demo, decisions, and risks into one document.</li>
    <li><strong>A quiz before merge</strong> — the owner must be able to explain the failure modes and the recovery path.</li>
</ol>

<h3>26. Final reading — what &ldquo;the bottleneck is the human&rdquo; really means</h3>

<p>Reading Thariq&rsquo;s argument as &ldquo;if the AI output is bad, it is the user&rsquo;s fault&rdquo; misses the point. It is rather the opposite. It is an argument that redefines what scarce resource is left to the human as models get better.</p>

<ul>
    <li>The ability to judge which problems are worth solving</li>
    <li>The ability to discover what you do not know</li>
    <li>The ability to externalize unspoken preferences and tacit rules</li>
    <li>The ability to track an agent&rsquo;s deviations from the plan and its assumptions</li>
    <li>The ability to understand and own the generated result well enough to operate it</li>
</ul>

<p>A strong model goes farther on fewer instructions. So making the prompt longer is not enough on its own. What is needed is <strong>a workflow that deliberately exposes the difference between the map and the territory</strong>. Thariq&rsquo;s 11 artifacts are concrete machinery for finding that difference before, during, and after implementation.</p>

<div class="note">
    <span class="nh">Compressed into one sentence</span>
    <strong>On your next project, do not put the AI straight to work — start by saying &ldquo;let&rsquo;s first find out together what I do not know.&rdquo;</strong>
</div>

